  "text": "**Solution 1 is the one to trust, though its set S is recalled from the literature rather than derived.**\n\nSolution 2 is not sound. Its central claim, that δ_χ(C_{2k+1},2) = 2/(2k+3) for every k, would make S infinite. The finite-set result I recall (Illingworth's work on minimum-degree stability of H-free graphs) says that for every 3-chromatic H, δ_χ(H,2) lies in {1/3, 2/5, 1/2}. Triangle-containing constructions block the small odd-cycle values for large k, so the odd-girth blow-up bound is not tight there. Solution 2's own lower-bound construction also doesn't establish its upper bounds: it cites a generalization of Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós without support for C_{2k+1}, k ≥ 2.\n\nSolution 1's values are plausible and consistent with the known examples. K_3 gives 2/5 (Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós), a 3-chromatic H with no colour-critical edge (such as the bowtie) gives 1/2 via K_{n/2,n/2}+e, and 1/3 is the remaining value in the recalled set. I have not verified that 1/3 is realized by some 3-chromatic H, so the set itself rests on my recollection of the theorem. Given that, the sum is 1/3 + 2/5 + 1/2 = 37/30.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
